1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,747
Now the server side: two processes and one RPC chain.

2
00:00:04,747 --> 00:00:10,263
The account service owns balances, the transaction service owns transfers and history,

3
00:00:10,263 --> 00:00:15,319
and it calls the account service for every balance it needs.

4
00:00:15,619 --> 00:00:19,162
The account service owns balances and nothing else.

5
00:00:19,162 --> 00:00:25,805
It has no idea what a transfer is, which is why it can be tested,

6
00:00:25,805 --> 00:00:28,462
scaled and replaced on its own.

7
00:00:28,462 --> 00:00:31,562
Three seeded accounts: alice, bob and carol.

8
00:00:31,562 --> 00:00:37,319
A real deployment swaps this dict for a database behind the same interface.

9
00:00:37,619 --> 00:00:40,241
This is the part that matters.

10
00:00:40,241 --> 00:00:47,233
gRPC serves requests on a thread pool, so two transfers can be in flight at once.

11
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:54,661
A read, a compare and a write is not atomic, so both could read the same balance,

12
00:00:54,661 --> 00:00:59,468
both pass the funds check, and the account ends up overdrawn.

13
00:00:59,468 --> 00:01:03,401
The lock around the whole operation makes that impossible.

14
00:01:03,701 --> 00:01:12,043
The store is the last line of defence: it refuses any update that would take the balance below zero,

15
00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:15,995
and it raises rather than returning a magic value.

16
00:01:15,995 --> 00:01:20,386
The service layer turns that raise into a status code.

17
00:01:20,386 --> 00:01:27,850
There is a test that fires fifty parallel credits and asserts the balance grew by exactly fifty.

18
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:33,145
Validation first: an empty user id is INVALID_ARGUMENT, not a lookup.

19
00:01:33,145 --> 00:01:35,416
An unknown account is NOT_FOUND.

20
00:01:35,416 --> 00:01:39,049
context.abort sends that code and the detail string,

21
00:01:39,049 --> 00:01:44,952
and on the client side the same call raises an error carrying both.

22
00:01:45,252 --> 00:01:47,517
math.isfinite catches NaN and infinity,

23
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:54,310
which is not paranoia: one NaN in a balance poisons every arithmetic result after it.

24
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:57,027
A zero delta is rejected too,

25
00:01:57,027 --> 00:02:02,462
because it is a no-op that would only pollute the audit log.

26
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:04,638
Three outcomes, three codes.

27
00:02:04,638 --> 00:02:05,838
It worked.

28
00:02:05,838 --> 00:02:08,651
The account does not exist, NOT_FOUND.

29
00:02:08,651 --> 00:02:11,933
Or the balance would go negative, FAILED_PRECONDITION.

30
00:02:11,933 --> 00:02:16,153
A client can branch on those without parsing text.

31
00:02:16,191 --> 00:02:19,824
The transaction service holds no balances, only history,

32
00:02:19,824 --> 00:02:23,002
and one channel created once and reused.

33
00:02:23,002 --> 00:02:28,452
Creating a channel per request would redo the HTTP/2 handshake every time.

34
00:02:28,452 --> 00:02:32,993
This process is now both a server and a client.

35
00:02:33,293 --> 00:02:39,800
When the account service says NOT_FOUND, this re-raises exactly that code to our caller.

36
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:47,238
Errors keep their meaning all the way through the chain instead of being flattened into INTERNAL.

37
00:02:47,538 --> 00:02:48,738
One: validate.

38
00:02:48,738 --> 00:02:53,895
Both ids present, not the same account, amount positive and finite.

39
00:02:53,895 --> 00:03:00,929
All of that happens before a single balance is read, so bad input costs nothing.

40
00:03:00,966 --> 00:03:07,198
Two: both accounts must exist, so it asks the account service rather than assuming,

41
00:03:07,198 --> 00:03:12,094
and a NOT_FOUND from there comes straight back to the caller.

42
00:03:12,094 --> 00:03:16,991
Three: read the sender's balance and compare it before moving anything.

43
00:03:16,991 --> 00:03:21,442
Not enough means FAILED_PRECONDITION and no state change at all.

44
00:03:21,742 --> 00:03:24,701
Then leg one: debit the sender.

45
00:03:24,701 --> 00:03:31,605
If that fails there is nothing to undo, so the error goes straight back.

46
00:03:31,905 --> 00:03:34,055
Leg two credits the receiver.

47
00:03:34,055 --> 00:03:41,794
Here is the honest part of a distributed system: these are two separate RPCs on two different keys,

48
00:03:41,794 --> 00:03:43,944
and no transaction spans them.

49
00:03:43,944 --> 00:03:46,954
If the credit fails, money has vanished.

50
00:03:46,954 --> 00:03:52,113
So I debit again with the opposite amount to put it back,

51
00:03:52,113 --> 00:03:54,263
then report the original error.

52
00:03:54,263 --> 00:04:01,142
If the rollback itself fails I log it as critical with the amount that is missing,

53
00:04:01,142 --> 00:04:05,442
because a silent inconsistency is worse than a loud one.

54
00:04:05,742 --> 00:04:12,200
One transfer writes two history rows sharing one transaction id: OUT for the sender,

55
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:14,045
IN for the receiver.

56
00:04:14,045 --> 00:04:19,580
Each row keeps balance_after from the response, so the statement is self-consistent.

57
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:20,780
Newest first.

58
00:04:20,803 --> 00:04:25,357
History confirms the account exists by asking the account service,

59
00:04:25,357 --> 00:04:31,732
so a real account with no activity returns an empty list rather than NOT_FOUND.

60
00:04:31,732 --> 00:04:37,197
Then it applies the limit with a hard cap of a hundred.

61
00:04:37,497 --> 00:04:41,553
A thread pool of ten on a plaintext port.

62
00:04:41,553 --> 00:04:48,313
In production this is add_secure_port with TLS credentials and the client uses a secure channel.

63
00:04:48,313 --> 00:04:55,523
I kept it plaintext so the demo is reproducible, and I list that as a limitation.

64
00:04:55,823 --> 00:05:02,419
This is the account log after one transfer: two balance reads, then a debit,

65
00:05:02,419 --> 00:05:03,833
then a credit.

66
00:05:03,833 --> 00:05:08,544
Four RPCs on this service for a single client call.

67
00:05:08,844 --> 00:05:14,717
And this is the transaction log: the transfer id, the direction of money,

68
00:05:14,717 --> 00:05:17,427
the resulting balances, and history reads.

69
00:05:17,427 --> 00:05:23,752
Reading the two logs side by side is how you debug a distributed call,

70
00:05:23,752 --> 00:05:26,462
because one transfer appears in both.

